<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rouen</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rotomagus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rouen is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, historic capital of Normandy. As Rotomagus it was the chief town of the Gallic Veliocasses and a Roman river port. Granted with the surrounding land to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rollo">Rollo</a> in 911, it became the capital of the Duchy of Normandy and one of the largest towns of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc">Joan of Arc</a> was tried and burned there in 1431, and its Gothic cathedral was painted many times by Monet.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rouen.jpg" alt="Rouen" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Herbert Frank from Wien (Vienna), AT · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rouen_(37903223574).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
